{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-123154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}=8,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =8{cos}^{2}⁡t-8{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =8({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+7}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{sin}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{sin}^{2}⁡t+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $8{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2\\sqrt{2},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+8{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $12{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+12{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(3-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+9}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $49{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $7,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+49{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=21\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }21\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+48}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+48}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123145" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{48{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/7fc64a261de4941b5ad35b8308b82b0f51c6b6bfbde177aafd793a0a0ac99da6.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=12,$ located in the second quadrant, shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/3bf746cfb21805587a65c7beeac90e75464f630bab1fbf3453de69ca3e6639eb.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4y}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{4y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+15}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-122269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/34b4b349b8295ee6443a85f019f6bf5e7ea6c6aafe9109ac7935610e6aefcacf.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $36{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=900,$ shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $5$ and $6,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=25-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =25-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =25-25{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =25(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =25{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(25-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }25{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "25{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "36{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "36{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡t+11}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{11{cos}^{2}⁡t+25}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/59296f25e2060b05f95ddc69f560e987f933d69f8966c1859ea0cd54bbb80175.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}=36,$ shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2(3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡t+16}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/1e09fa361aca812f22d9d28430a8584e069c0eb9a2aa90f5e665d892dddd378f.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(5-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is three-quarters of the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5,$ shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{5},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+5{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5-(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =5-5{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =5(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =5{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(5-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}5{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123304" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/570b00c901daedddc9fe05855e2df5fb2c24158320544b955bda651210d9f803.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}16xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9$  traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-3,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=16xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =16\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=48\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}16xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }48\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+8{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{9}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{9}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3/2}-{9}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(1-27) \\\\  & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-34"] ["math" "-48"] ["math" "-52"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "56"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-52"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9xy$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $\\frac{4}{3},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\frac{16}{9}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=48\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }48\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=8{sin}^{2}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{128}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123281" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/f1a234c8ec4ac3e2dcc4c2bfd2232c006179a3ca7597c05afbb4243435eca387.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}7xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=144$  traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(4,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $4,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7{sin}^{2}⁡t+(9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+7{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=84\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}7xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }84\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{9+7{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =84{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{9+7{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=9+7{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{9+7{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{16}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{16}^{9} \\\\  & =4({9}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =4(27-64) \\\\  & =-148\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-148"] ["math" "-42"] ["math" "-64"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "84"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-148"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy(3+2{y}^{2})$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $\\sqrt{3},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{sin}^{2}⁡t+3({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy(3+2{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (3+2(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})=3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,(3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(3+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{(3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{(3+6{sin}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{3}^{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/f3776aa331fba6b94bf24ebe254010d9b5954ba2a8edb6635be658e38bbc4039.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}8xy\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=5$  traversed from the point $(\\sqrt{5},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $\\sqrt{5}$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=8xy\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =8\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}=8\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}8xy\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1+4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({5}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}(25-1) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff98aeb61e063f6a15a0e6e2cd3791eed18b3e7d7f16208e479a0de130362143"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "12\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "q-123205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/state.json" "file_sha256" "7d6bfe1760b9db760e163ba7219a0300209fc05ed3b58a04ebb918d001fb0e4e"} {"question" "e-9460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9460.json" "file_sha256" "b2c446e19d7b2e61a2f1f8849f622e9a34942f46852cb9fe393724672aec1670"} {"question" "e-9460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9460.json" "file_sha256" "b2c446e19d7b2e61a2f1f8849f622e9a34942f46852cb9fe393724672aec1670"} {"question" "e-9488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/71df4fc7bd52fe5dd97c7e01502dec2ad68ae31fdf224cccd812c6b7318c70b7.png)\n\nFind, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9488.json" "file_sha256" "9e79b6ab5d1cb404bd6efdabf93b8b24e8d2fcefe35bfda74e9f09e454a4886a"} {"question" "e-9488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9488.json" "file_sha256" "9e79b6ab5d1cb404bd6efdabf93b8b24e8d2fcefe35bfda74e9f09e454a4886a"} {"question" "e-9439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/fda8bfb0533b86dc4050d1b8e309e332996b26263b1052d3e251bf0a5c98e816.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0),$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9439.json" "file_sha256" "19cd00cbe08d56cb75be4ac7946020d7f7354a854ed4e2072f49c9f3801cec80"} {"question" "e-9439" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2}  = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 12xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}12xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3\\cos^2 {t},$ $\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos \n t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/example-9439.json" "file_sha256" "19cd00cbe08d56cb75be4ac7946020d7f7354a854ed4e2072f49c9f3801cec80"}] "usage" {"e-9439" [] "e-9488" [] "e-9460" [] "q-122269" []} "basis" 1857 "retractions" [[17592186179893 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/q-122269.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $∫_(C)\\frac{4y}{x}ds$, where the curve $C$ is the left half of the ellipse $x^{2} + 16y^{2} = 16$, shown above?"] [17592186179508 :question/problem "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [17592186179508 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-4\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{4\\cos t}{\\sin t} = 4\\cot t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x}{y}ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}4\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179518 :question/problem "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/372d3096b2e6ec488c80ec89998d7543.png)"] [17592186179518 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 3\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (3\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 9\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2xy\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t)(3\\sin t) \\\\\n&= 12\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)2xyds &= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}12\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt \\\\\n&= 12∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179503 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/68d8dbac3c41c99668f956642b8e26fd.png)"] [17592186179503 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3\\cos^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 12xy\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 12 \\cdot \\cos t \\cdot 2\\sin t = 24\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)12xyds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}24\\sin t\\cos t \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+3\\cos^2 {t}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt &= -4∫_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2} \\mid _{1}^{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(8 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{56}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177359 17592186177360 17592186177361 17592186177362 17592186177363] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177358 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177358 :answer-field/id] [17592186177358 :answer-field/key] [17592186177358 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
